name: disks and filesystems
boss: Phantom
flavor: Every privilege is a door I already opened.
brief: A disk is a block device. A filesystem is a structure written onto it.
brief: mount attaches that structure somewhere in the tree; until you mount
brief: it, a disk is just bytes.
brief:
brief: On this machine the root is a read-only squashfs verified block by block,
brief: and the host's own drives are deliberately invisible -- there are no
brief: NVMe or SATA drivers compiled in. What you can see is the stick you
brief: booted from and anything on USB.
---
q: {show how much space is free on the mounted filesystems|how much free space is left on the mounted filesystems|report the free space on every mounted filesystem}
a: df
out: [0-9]
ref: df
teach: df
hint: df alone reports every mounted filesystem, one line each
---
q: %show% free space in a human readable form
a: df -h
out: [0-9]
ref: df
teach: df -h
hint: -h scales the numbers to k/m/g instead of raw block counts
---
q: %show% free space in megabytes
a: df -m
out: [0-9]
ref: df
teach: df -m
hint: -m forces megabytes specifically, no auto-scaling
---
q: %show% inode usage rather than block usage
a: df -i
out: [0-9]
ref: df
teach: df -i
hint: -i swaps the space columns for inode counts and percent-used
---
q: %show% free space including pseudo-filesystems normally hidden
a: df -a
out: [0-9]
ref: df
teach: df -a
hint: -a includes filesystems df otherwise skips, like proc and tmpfs
---
q: %show% free space with the filesystem type in a column
a: df -T
out: [0-9]
ref: df
teach: df -T
hint: -T adds a column naming each filesystem's type
---
q: %show% free space only for filesystems of type squashfs
a: df -t squashfs
ref: df
teach: df -t
hint: -t filters the listing down to one filesystem type
---
q: %show% free space in units of 1024 bytes, chosen explicitly
a: df -B 1024
out: [0-9]
ref: df
teach: df -B
hint: -B takes a size -- here 1024 -- to fix the unit df reports in
---
q: {how much space does @dir@ use in total|what is the total space used by @dir@|report the total size of @dir@}
a: du -s @dir@
out: [0-9]
ref: du
teach: du -s
hint: -s collapses the whole tree into one summary line
---
q: {how much space does @dir@ use, in a human readable form|what does @dir@ use, scaled to something readable|report the size of @dir@ in human readable units}
a: du -sh @dir@
out: [0-9]
ref: du
teach: du -h
hint: -h scales the summary size to k/m/g
---
q: %show% the size of every file under @dir@, not just the directories
a: du -a @dir@
out: [0-9]
ref: du
teach: du -a
hint: -a walks down to individual files, not just directories
---
q: %show% sizes in bytes rather than blocks
a: du -b @dir@
out: [0-9]
ref: du
teach: du -b
hint: -b reports raw bytes instead of filesystem blocks
---
q: %show% @dir@ only one level deep
a: du -d 1 @dir@
out: [0-9]
ref: du
teach: du -d
hint: -d takes a depth -- 1 stops descending after the first level
---
q: %show% a grand total at the end
a: du -c @dir@
out: total
ref: du
teach: du -c
hint: -c appends one extra 'total' line summing everything above it
---
q: %show% sizes in megabytes
a: du -m @dir@
out: [0-9]
ref: du
teach: du -m
hint: -m forces megabytes
---
q: %show% sizes in kilobytes explicitly
a: du -k @dir@
out: [0-9]
ref: du
teach: du -k
hint: -k forces kilobytes, explicit rather than du's default
---
q: {measure @dir@ without crossing onto another filesystem|measure @dir@ but stay on its own filesystem|report the size of @dir@ without descending into other mounts}
a: du -x @dir@
out: [0-9]
ref: du
teach: du -x
hint: -x stops du from following mounts onto other filesystems
---
q: %find% the ten largest things under here
a: du -a . | sort -rn | head -10
out: [0-9]
ref: du
teach: du -a
hint: du -a lists everything with a size; sort -rn and head -10 do the ranking
---
q: %list% the block devices this machine can see, with their filesystem labels
a: blkid
ref: blkid
teach: blkid
hint: blkid reads filesystem superblocks for uuid, label and type -- no mount needed
---
q: %find% which device holds the filesystem labelled XOS-ROOT
a: findfs LABEL=XOS-ROOT
ref: findfs
teach: findfs
hint: findfs takes a LABEL= or UUID= and prints the matching device node
---
q: {mount /dev/sdb1 read-only at /mnt|mount /dev/sdb1 at /mnt, allowing no writes|attach /dev/sdb1 at /mnt without write access}
a: mount -r /dev/sdb1 /mnt
ref: mount
teach: mount -r
hint: on a machine you do not trust, read-only is the default you want
---
q: {mount /dev/sdb1 at /mnt naming the filesystem type explicitly|mount /dev/sdb1 at /mnt, telling mount the filesystem type instead of letting it guess|explicitly name the filesystem type while mounting /dev/sdb1 at /mnt}
a: mount -t ext4 /dev/sdb1 /mnt
ref: mount
teach: mount -t
hint: -t names the filesystem type explicitly instead of letting mount guess
---
q: {mount /dev/sdb1 at /mnt with nosuid, nodev and noexec|mount /dev/sdb1 at /mnt, disallowing setuid, device nodes and execution|attach /dev/sdb1 at /mnt with the nosuid, nodev and noexec options set}
a: mount -o nosuid,nodev,noexec /dev/sdb1 /mnt
a: mount -o noexec,nodev,nosuid /dev/sdb1 /mnt
ref: mount
teach: mount -o
hint: -o takes a comma-separated list of mount options
---
q: %show% what is currently mounted
a: mount
out: /
ref: mount
teach: mount
hint: mount with no arguments lists what is currently mounted
---
q: {unmount /mnt|detach whatever is mounted at /mnt|take /mnt out of the mounted tree}
a: umount /mnt
ref: umount
teach: umount
hint: umount takes the mountpoint, or the device, and detaches it
---
q: {unmount /mnt even though something is still using it, detaching it lazily|detach /mnt lazily, even with something still using it|unmount /mnt now but let the detach finish once it is no longer busy}
a: umount -l /mnt
ref: umount
teach: umount -l
hint: -l detaches it now and cleans up once nothing is using it
---
q: {force an unmount of /mnt|force /mnt to unmount|make /mnt unmount even if it resists}
a: umount -f /mnt
ref: umount
teach: umount -f
hint: -f forces the unmount even if the filesystem is unresponsive
---
q: {unmount everything that can be unmounted|detach every filesystem that will allow it|unmount as much as possible in one go}
a: umount -a
ref: umount
teach: umount -a
hint: -a unmounts everything listed in /proc/mounts it can
---
q: {attach the file disk.img to a loop device so it can be mounted|make disk.img mountable by attaching it to a loop device|wire disk.img up to a loop device so it behaves like a block device}
a: losetup -f disk.img
ref: losetup
teach: losetup -f
hint: -f finds the next free loop device and attaches to it in one step
---
q: {attach disk.img read-only to a loop device|loop-attach disk.img without allowing writes|attach disk.img to a loop device in read-only mode}
a: losetup -r -f disk.img
ref: losetup
teach: losetup -r
hint: -r attaches the loop device read-only
---
q: {attach disk.img starting at an offset of 1 megabyte|loop-attach disk.img, skipping the first megabyte|attach disk.img to a loop device, starting 1 megabyte into the file}
a: losetup -o 1048576 -f disk.img
ref: losetup
teach: losetup -o
hint: -o takes a byte offset into the image to start the mapping at
---
q: {attach disk.img and scan it for partitions|loop-attach disk.img and have it scanned for partitions|attach disk.img, exposing any partitions it contains}
a: losetup -P -f disk.img
ref: losetup
teach: losetup -P
hint: -P scans the attached image for a partition table
---
q: %list% the partition table of /dev/sda
a: fdisk -l /dev/sda
ref: fdisk
teach: fdisk -l
hint: -l just lists the partition table, no interactive prompt
---
q: {report the block size of /dev/sda|what is the block size of /dev/sda|find the block size of /dev/sda}
a: blockdev --getbsz /dev/sda
ref: blockdev
teach: blockdev --getbsz
hint: --getbsz reports the block size
---
q: {report the size of /dev/sda in bytes|what is the size of /dev/sda, in bytes|find the exact byte size of /dev/sda}
a: blockdev --getsize64 /dev/sda
ref: blockdev
teach: blockdev --getsize64
hint: --getsize64 reports the size in bytes, not sectors
---
q: {mark /dev/sda read-only at the block layer|flip /dev/sda to read-only below the filesystem layer|set the block-level read-only flag on /dev/sda}
a: blockdev --setro /dev/sda
ref: blockdev
teach: blockdev --setro
hint: --setro flips the read-only flag at the block layer, below any filesystem
---
q: {ask whether /dev/sda is currently read-only|check whether /dev/sda's read-only flag is set|is /dev/sda currently read-only at the block layer}
a: blockdev --getro /dev/sda
ref: blockdev
teach: blockdev --getro
hint: --getro reads that same read-only flag back
---
q: {flush everything buffered in memory out to disk|force every dirty buffer out to disk right now|write all pending buffered data to disk}
a: sync
ref: sync
teach: sync
hint: sync with no arguments flushes every dirty buffer on the system
---
q: {flush only the data for @txt@, not the whole system|flush just @txt@'s buffered data, leaving the rest alone|write out only @txt@'s pending data, not every buffer on the system}
a: sync -d @txt@
ref: sync
teach: sync -d
hint: -d limits the flush to the file you name
---
q: {run a command holding an exclusive lock on lock.file, so two copies cannot overlap|hold an exclusive lock on lock.file while a command runs, so it cannot overlap another copy|use lock.file as an exclusive lock around a command so two copies never run together}
a: flock -x lock.file echo held
out: held
ref: flock
teach: flock -x
hint: -x is flock's default, an exclusive lock -- named here for clarity
---
q: {take the lock but give up immediately if someone else holds it|try the lock and bail out at once if it is already held|attempt lock.file's lock, failing instantly rather than waiting}
a: flock -n lock.file echo held
out: held
ref: flock
teach: flock -n
hint: -n makes flock fail instead of waiting when the lock is held
---
q: {take a shared lock rather than an exclusive one|take lock.file's lock as shared, not exclusive|hold a shared lock instead of an exclusive one}
a: flock -s lock.file echo held
out: held
ref: flock
teach: flock -s
hint: -s takes a shared lock, which can coexist with other shared locks
---
setup: p3 is the state partition -- the only thing on this stick that survives
setup: a reboot, and the only place a key can live. It is encrypted, and it is
setup: also authenticated, which is a different property and the harder one.
q: {write a new LUKS2 header on /dev/sda3, with authentication as well as encryption|format /dev/sda3 with a LUKS2 header that is both encrypted and authenticated|put a new LUKS2 header on /dev/sda3, adding integrity checking alongside the encryption}
a: cryptsetup luksFormat --type luks2 --integrity hmac-sha256 /dev/sda3
a: cryptsetup luksFormat --integrity hmac-sha256 --type luks2 /dev/sda3
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --type
teach: cryptsetup --integrity
hint: encryption stops them reading it; integrity stops them changing it
---
q: {unlock /dev/sda3 and expose it as /dev/mapper/state|open /dev/sda3 and map it as /dev/mapper/state|decrypt /dev/sda3, exposing it under /dev/mapper as state}
a: cryptsetup open /dev/sda3 state
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup
hint: open maps the decrypted device under /dev/mapper by the name you give
---
q: {unlock it without allowing any writes|open /dev/sda3 read-only, allowing no writes through it|decrypt /dev/sda3 without permitting any writes}
a: cryptsetup open --readonly /dev/sda3 state
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --readonly
hint: --readonly maps the device without allowing writes through it
---
q: {lock it again and remove the mapping|close the state mapping and re-lock the device|tear down /dev/mapper/state and lock /dev/sda3 again}
a: cryptsetup close state
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup
hint: close tears down the /dev/mapper entry and re-locks the device
---
q: {is state currently open, and with what cipher|check whether state is open, and which cipher it is using|report state's current status and cipher}
a: cryptsetup status state
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup
hint: status reports whether it's active and which cipher it's using
---
q: %show% the LUKS header of /dev/sda3 -- cipher, key slots and kdf
a: cryptsetup luksDump /dev/sda3
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup
hint: luksDump reads the header itself -- it never touches the encrypted data
---
q: {format /dev/sda3 choosing the cipher explicitly|format /dev/sda3, naming the cipher instead of taking the default|choose the cipher yourself while formatting /dev/sda3}
a: cryptsetup luksFormat --cipher aes-xts-plain64 /dev/sda3
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --cipher
hint: --cipher names the cipher instead of taking cryptsetup's default
---
q: {format with a 512 bit key|format /dev/sda3 using a 512 bit key|choose a 512 bit key while formatting /dev/sda3}
a: cryptsetup luksFormat --key-size 512 /dev/sda3
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --key-size
hint: --key-size is in bits, and must match what the cipher supports
---
q: {format choosing pbkdf2 rather than the argon2id default|format /dev/sda3 with pbkdf2 instead of the argon2id default|swap the key derivation function to pbkdf2 while formatting}
a: cryptsetup luksFormat --pbkdf pbkdf2 /dev/sda3
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --pbkdf
hint: --pbkdf picks the key derivation function used to slow down password guessing
---
q: {format without any prompt or confirmation, for a script|format /dev/sda3 with no confirmation prompt, suitable for a script|skip the are-you-sure prompt while formatting, for scripted use}
a: cryptsetup luksFormat --batch-mode /dev/sda3
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --batch-mode
hint: --batch-mode skips the are-you-sure prompt, for scripting
---
q: {open a device saying out loud what is happening|open /dev/sda3 while narrating what cryptsetup is doing|unlock the device with verbose output describing each step}
a: cryptsetup open --verbose /dev/sda3 state
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --verbose
hint: --verbose narrates what cryptsetup is doing as it opens the device
---
q: {mount the unlocked state device with nothing on it able to run|mount /dev/mapper/state so nothing on it can execute|attach the decrypted state device with execution disabled}
a: mount -o nosuid,nodev,noexec /dev/mapper/state /state
a: mount -o noexec,nodev,nosuid /dev/mapper/state /state
ref: mount
teach: mount -o
hint: same nosuid,nodev,noexec trio, now applied to the decrypted volume
---
setup: A disk someone hands you is untrusted input, and a filesystem driver is
setup: a parser that runs it in your kernel. The safest read of a disk you do
setup: not trust is dd at the block level, where no parser runs at all.
q: {image the whole removable disk /dev/sda to a file, reading raw bytes with no filesystem|make a raw byte-for-byte image of the removable disk /dev/sda, with no filesystem involved|read /dev/sda straight into a file, bypassing any filesystem parser}
a: dd if=@txt@ of=disk.img
state: exists:disk.img
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: no mount, no parser -- just the bytes. this is the forensically safe read
---
q: {mount a fat32 usb stick read-only, with nothing on it able to run|mount a fat32 usb stick read-only, with execution disabled|attach a fat32 usb stick read-only and let nothing on it run}
a: mount -t vfat -o ro,nosuid,nodev,noexec /dev/sda1 /mnt
a: mount -t vfat -o noexec,nodev,nosuid,ro /dev/sda1 /mnt
ref: mount
teach: mount -t
hint: ro, and noexec, because you do not trust what is on it
---
q: {mount an exfat sd card read-only and safely|safely mount an exfat sd card read-only|attach an exfat sd card read-only, locked down the same way}
a: mount -t exfat -o ro,nosuid,nodev,noexec /dev/sda1 /mnt
a: mount -t exfat -o noexec,nodev,nosuid,ro /dev/sda1 /mnt
ref: mount
teach: mount -t
hint: same ro,nosuid,nodev,noexec pattern, this time for exfat
---
q: {mount a windows ntfs disk read-only|mount a windows ntfs disk allowing no writes|attach an ntfs disk in read-only mode}
a: mount -t ntfs3 -o ro,nosuid,nodev,noexec /dev/sda2 /mnt
a: mount -t ntfs3 -o noexec,nodev,nosuid,ro /dev/sda2 /mnt
ref: mount
teach: mount -t
hint: read only -- xos will not write ntfs, by design
---
q: {loop-mount an iso image read-only without burning it|mount an iso image read-only through a loop device, no burning required|attach an iso image as a read-only loop mount}
a: mount -t iso9660 -o ro,loop image.iso /mnt
ref: mount
teach: mount -t
hint: -o ro,loop reads the iso straight off the image file, no burn needed
---
q: {attach disk.img to a loop device first, then you can mount or examine it|before mounting or examining disk.img, attach it to a loop device|loop-attach disk.img so it can be mounted or examined afterward}
a: losetup -f disk.img
ref: losetup
teach: losetup -f
hint: the same -f as before -- find a free loop device and attach here
---
q: %make% an ext filesystem on the unlocked state device
a: mke2fs /dev/mapper/state
ref: mke2fs
teach: mke2fs
hint: mke2fs with no flags builds an ext2 filesystem by default
---
q: %make% an ext filesystem without being asked to confirm
a: mke2fs -F /dev/mapper/state
ref: mke2fs
teach: mke2fs -F
hint: -F skips the confirmation prompt, useful for scripting
---
q: %make% an ext filesystem giving it a label
a: mke2fs -L data /dev/mapper/state
ref: mke2fs
teach: mke2fs -L
hint: -L sets the filesystem's volume label
---
q: {reserve no blocks for admin, so all space is usable, when making the filesystem|when making the filesystem, reserve zero blocks for admin use|build the filesystem with no space reserved for the superuser}
a: mke2fs -m 0 /dev/mapper/state
ref: mke2fs
teach: mke2fs -m
hint: -m sets the percent reserved for root; 0 reserves nothing
---
q: {format a usb stick partition as fat32|make a fat32 filesystem on a usb stick partition|turn a usb stick partition into fat32}
a: mkdosfs /dev/sda1
ref: mkdosfs
teach: mkdosfs
hint: mkdosfs with no flags builds fat32 (or fat16, sized by the partition)
---
q: {format a fat filesystem with a volume label|make a fat filesystem, giving it a volume label|format a fat filesystem and label it while you're at it}
a: mkdosfs -n BACKUP /dev/sda1
ref: mkdosfs
teach: mkdosfs -n
hint: -n sets the volume label
